## std::fmt — Display + Debug formal mixins, plus the placeholder
## Formatter / FmtError types.
##
## Wave 2 (#06.8) — migrated from resolve/stdlib/mod.rs Rust
## registrations into self-hosted library/std/src/fmt.rx.
##
## Display is the canonical interpolation mixin (`"#{x}"` lowers
## through Display::fmt). Debug is the `"#{x:?}"` and `derive Debug`
## target. Both share the `fmt(&self, &mut Formatter) ->
## Result[(), FmtError]` signature, but the Rust registration only
## carried the method NAME (`["fmt"]`) — typeck checks
## `impl Display for T` / `impl Debug for T` provides `fmt` against
## that name. The minimum-faithful mixin definitions below preserve
## that contract; the broader generic-signature work lands when
## mixin default-method lowering does.
##
## Formatter and FmtError are placeholder classes (no fields, no
## methods registered through DefKind::Method yet). The runtime
## `ruxen_fmt_*` helpers in library/runtime/fmt.c back the
## constructor + dispatch wired by Phase D's lower_interpolation.
## v1 deliberately keeps the public surface empty — see
## docs/specs/stdlib/fmt.spec.md.

mixin Display
  def fmt
end

mixin Debug
  def fmt
end

## std::fmt::Formatter — buffered accumulator that `Display::fmt` /
## `Debug::fmt` write into. Phase E-rest 3 of #06.95 migrated the
## constructor + low-level write surface from `runtime_table` arms
## into class-body lib decls. `new_with_spec` and `precision` stay
## on runtime_table because their `Option[USize]` typeck contract
## doesn't yet round-trip through the FFI alias path (the C runtime
## uses an `int64_t -1` sentinel for None, which the lib decl
## syntax can't yet express).
##
## FmtError is declared BEFORE Formatter so Formatter.write_str /
## write_char's `-> Result[(), FmtError]` return type resolves at
## parse time.
class FmtError
end

class Formatter
  lib "runtime/fmt.c"
    def self.new as "ruxen_fmt_formatter_new"() -> Formatter
    def free as "ruxen_fmt_formatter_free"(self) -> ()
    def write_str as "ruxen_fmt_formatter_write_str"(self, s: &String) -> Result[(), FmtError]
    # `write_char` takes Char at the Ruxen surface but the C runtime
    # signature is `(RuxenFormatter*, int32_t)` — Char emits as i32
    # but the call site passes i64. Widening the lib decl to Int
    # keeps the cranelift verifier happy; the C function silently
    # truncates to int32 (UTF-32 codepoints fit in 21 bits anyway).
    def write_char as "ruxen_fmt_formatter_write_char"(self, ch: Int) -> Result[(), FmtError]
    def buffer as "ruxen_fmt_formatter_buffer"(self) -> String
    def len as "ruxen_fmt_formatter_len"(self) -> Int
  end
end
